  "tour": {
    "title": "John’s New Creation: Genesis Echoes in the Fourth Gospel",
    "intro": "This tour listens for the Genesis-shaped architecture of John’s Gospel and follows it into Jesus’ teaching, signs, and resurrection. It moves from John 1’s light, life, and tabernacle imagery to new birth, the healing of blind humanity, and finally the “eighth day” of resurrection and the Spirit’s commission.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:beginning",
        "startSec": 520,
        "endSec": 880,
        "why": "Start with John’s architecture. The speakers say the prologue tells Jesus’ story first through “the language of Genesis 1” and then through “the language of the tabernacle.” That gives you two main Genesis-related lenses—creation and God’s dwelling presence—before you hear how the rest of the Gospel develops them.",
        "episodeTitle": "In the Beginning – John 1",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/8f0c5975-65cf-4a02-baf0-786ba6649656/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=8f0c5975-65cf-4a02-baf0-786ba6649656",
        "durationSec": 3671,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:4613377c-3e5d-11f1-b7fb-27eb7613d571",
        "startSec": 340,
        "endSec": 700,
        "why": "Now move from structure to meaning. The speakers say, “John begins his gospel with Eden, with new creation,” and trace darkness, light, water, Spirit, Adam, and Jesus as the “new Adam.” Their key proposal is that “life itself has become us,” so Jesus’ incarnation is how John describes God healing and remaking creation.",
        "episodeTitle": "John 1: In the Beginning Was the Word",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG8007534722.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3919,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:anointed-question-and-response",
        "startSec": 220,
        "endSec": 400,
        "why": "This clip takes the Genesis echo into Jesus’ conversation with Nicodemus. It explains the double sense of being born “again” or “from above,” then connects “born of water and the spirit” with the Garden of Eden and with Isaiah’s promise that God will pour out both water and Spirit to bring new life. It shows that John’s allusion is a network, not just a reference to one verse.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Anointed Question and Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/44966189-3eae-410f-aded-16b3ec4f2e71/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=44966189-3eae-410f-aded-16b3ec4f2e71",
        "durationSec": 3564,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:db54663c-6a8e-11f1-8d65-4723e9decce5",
        "startSec": 3840,
        "endSec": 4160,
        "why": "Here the proposal becomes visible in one of John’s signs: Jesus makes mud, sends the blind man to wash, and gives him sight. The speakers call it “new creation” and say, “God is reenacting the moment of humanity’s creation in Eden there in the dirt.” They also connect the man’s new sight back to Nicodemus: Jesus must “create new creation in you to make you see.”",
        "episodeTitle": "John Overview: Living Water",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG6305125345.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4717,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:e1d1cb1c-1ba2-11f0-9e6a-730b0b3fac80",
        "startSec": 620,
        "endSec": 760,
        "why": "Finish with John 20, where the Genesis pattern reaches resurrection and mission. Wright points out John’s repeated “first day of the week” and calls it “the beginning of God’s new creation,” or “the eighth day.” He then links Jesus breathing the Spirit onto the disciples with God breathing life into Adam and explains how the new-creation people are sent to do for the world what Jesus did.",
        "episodeTitle": "BONUS Subscriber only Easter Show: What does Easter mean for the mission of the Church today? Tom Wright and Mike Bird",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR4249819305.mp3?updated=1749648274",
        "durationSec": 1578,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips present John’s “new creation” as a Genesis-shaped retelling: light and life enter darkness, God dwells among humans, water and Spirit bring new birth, Jesus re-forms humanity, and resurrection opens the eighth day. Listen especially for the way the teachers describe these as layered echoes—Genesis 1, Genesis 2, tabernacle, prophetic restoration, and John 20 all converging on what Jesus is doing.",
    "totalSeconds": 1360
  },
